/* book Worked example: Q: A path is inverter → NAND2 → inverter. Its input capacitance is C (a unit inverter) and it drives 64C. There is no branching. Find F, f̂, D and the input capacitance of each stage, and the transistor widths of the NAND2.
   1. N = 3. G = 1 × 4/3 × 1 = 4/3. H = 64C/C = 64. B = 1.
   2. F = GBH = (4/3) × 64 = 85.33.
   3. f̂ = 85.331/3 = 4.403.
   4. P = 1 + 2 + 1 = 4, so D = 3 × 4.403 + 4 = 17.2τ.
   5. Last inverter: Cin3 = 1 × 64C/4.403 = 14.54C. NAND2: Cin2 = (4/3) × 14.54C/4.403 = 4.40C. First inverter: Cin1 = 4.40C/4.403 = 1.00C, which matches the given input, a useful check.
   6. Widths: a unit NAND2 input is 4 units (nMOS 2, pMOS 2) and C = 3 units, so 4.40C = 13.2 units, 3.30 times the unit NAND2. Each nMOS and pMOS is about 6.6 unit widths. The last inverter is 14.54 times a unit inverter: nMOS 14.5, pMOS 29.1.
   7. Stage delays check: each stage has f = 4.40, so d = 5.40, 6.40 and 5.40τ, total 17.2τ.
   Answer: D ≈ 17.2τ with input capacitances 1C, 4.40C and 14.54C (NAND2 transistors about 6.6 units wide, last inverter 14.5/29.1). */
