/* book Worked example: Q: A copper wire on an intermediate metal layer is L = 1 mm long, W = 0.2 µm wide and t = 0.4 µm thick, with an effective resistivity of 2.2 µΩ·cm. It sits h = 0.3 µm above the layer below, in silicon dioxide (εr = 3.9). A field solver gives fringe capacitance 0.04 fF/µm per edge and coupling 0.05 fF/µm to each of its two neighbours. Find R□, Rw, Cpp and Cw (neighbours quiet).
   1. R□ = ρ/t = 2.2 × 10⁻⁸ Ω·m / 0.4 × 10⁻⁶ m = 0.055 Ω/□.
   2. Squares: L/W = 1000 µm / 0.2 µm = 5000.
   3. Rw = 0.055 × 5000 = 275 Ω.
   4. Cpp = 3.9 × 8.854 × 10⁻¹² × (0.2 × 10⁻⁶ × 10⁻³)/(0.3 × 10⁻⁶) = 2.30 × 10⁻¹⁴ F = 23.0 fF.
   5. Fringe: 2 edges × 0.04 fF/µm × 1000 µm = 80 fF. Coupling: 2 neighbours × 0.05 fF/µm × 1000 µm = 100 fF.
   6. Cw = 23.0 + 80 + 100 = 203 fF, that is 0.20 fF/µm. The plate term is only 11% of it.
   7. If both neighbours switch the opposite way, the coupling counts double: Cw = 23 + 80 + 200 = 303 fF.
   Answer: R□ = 0.055 Ω/□, Rw = 275 Ω, Cpp = 23 fF and Cw ≈ 203 fF (303 fF with both neighbours switching against it). */
