/* book exam traps: Using L × W instead of L/W for squares. Resistance grows with length and falls with width. | Keeping only the parallel-plate term. Fringe and coupling dominate in narrow wires. | Using 0.69RC for a distributed line. Its 50% delay is 0.38RC; 0.69RC is the lumped value. | Forgetting that wire delay grows with L², so halving the length quarters it. | Calling inductance negligible for every wire. Wide clock and power wires with fast edges need an RLC model. */
